# Docs: API examples must be executable truth

When editing Sphinx sources or demo scripts that teach Python / registry
calls, **the running code is the authority** — not sklearn memory, old YAML
field names, or “looks familiar”.

## Authority order (do not invert)

1. `SomeRegistry.params_model("name")` field names, or the component’s
   `__init__` / params model in `habit/domain/**`
2. Then write the doc / demo snippet
3. Never invent synonyms (`n_clusters` / `n_components` for supervoxelizers
   when the model field is `n_supervoxels`)

## Protocols are optional, not universal

- `Seedable.set_random_state` exists only on components that implement it
  (e.g. kmeans/gmm supervoxelizers and stochastic fitters). **SLIC has no
  `set_random_state`** — do not call it on `slic`.
- Before documenting a method call, confirm with `hasattr(obj, "...")` or
  the class body. Name the concrete component in the example.

## Done gate for taught API snippets

If you add or change a `code-block` / demo that contains
`Registry.create(`, `set_random_state(`, or a public recipe call users will
copy:

1. Run the critical lines under `py310`
   (`E:\conda\mconda\envs\py310\python.exe`) with synthetic data when needed.
2. Sphinx green alone is **not** enough (rst `code-block` is not doctest).

## When editing a page that already has examples

Touching a nearby section is not enough: re-check other
`create(` / method calls **on the same page** so stale wrong examples do
not ship next to new text.

## Out of scope here

- Shell line-continuations → `docs-windows-shell.mdc`
- gh-pages deploy → `docs-gh-pages-deploy.mdc`
- YAML config keys under `configuration/` may still use v0.1 names
  (`n_clusters`); that is schema translation, not `Registry.create` kwargs.
